WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.533
[HORSE HOOVES CLOPPING]

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:06.189
ALYSSA GOODMAN: In 1854, cholera
struck the city of London.

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:09.929
Over 600 people died
in just a few weeks.

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:13.799
Physician John Snow is often
credited with discovering

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:18.319
that cholera is a waterborne
disease and with ending the 1854

00:00:18.319 --> 00:00:24.399
epidemic by removing the handle from
the contaminated Broad Street Pump.

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:27.425
The full story is much more interesting.

00:00:27.425 --> 00:00:31.709
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:35.993 --> 00:00:39.679
So I've been reading about
the John Snow Pub for years.

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:40.739
There it is.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:41.909
Look at that.

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:47.099
Right here, on what used to be Broad
Street, is this fine-looking pub.

00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:50.029
There's this very inauspicious sign.

00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:53.179
It says here, "The Red Granite
kerbstone," over there,

00:00:53.179 --> 00:00:55.059
"marks the site of the
historic Broad Street

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:59.379
Pump associated with Dr.
John Snow's discovery in 1854

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:02.089
that Cholera is conveyed by water."

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:06.619
So that's a really simple summary
of the whole John Snow story.

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:09.629
And to give you just an
overview, here's the story.

00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:11.079
So it's 1854.

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:14.319
It's really hot and the end
of the summer in London.

00:01:14.319 --> 00:01:18.194
We're here is Soho, which was a
very crowded neighborhood filled

00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:21.759
of tenements with people
not of a lot of means.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:24.929
And there were a lot of people
crammed into these buildings--

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:26.549
many, many people to an apartment.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:28.144
And it's hot, and it's sweaty.

00:01:28.144 --> 00:01:31.269
And the part that you can't imagine,
looking right now-- because it's still

00:01:31.269 --> 00:01:34.629
pretty crowded; it's not that hot,
but it's still pretty crowded-- here,

00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:36.419
the street now is completely clean.

00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:38.419
We see street sweeping vehicles.

00:01:38.419 --> 00:01:39.979
We see drainage in the streets.

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:40.939
We have sewers.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:42.546
None of that was true in 1854.

00:01:42.546 --> 00:01:45.129
Instead, the people who lived
in these buildings, some of them

00:01:45.129 --> 00:01:47.581
had cesspools in these
little front courtyards.

00:01:47.581 --> 00:01:49.789
And they would take their
human waste and other waste

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:52.289
and just kind of throw it out
the window into the cesspool

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:54.829
or bring it down to the cesspool.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:57.959
And then it would drain
wherever it drained.

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:00.529
And the street-- whoa--
was not something

00:02:00.529 --> 00:02:03.009
that you could easily walk
on and keep your feet clean.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:06.479
Wow, that's slippery and disgusting.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:11.343
And so people were used to this kind
of somewhat disgusting ambiance.

00:02:11.343 --> 00:02:14.259
And people thought that when there
were terrible outbreaks of disease,

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:18.899
especially cholera, that it was
caused by the very poisonous air

00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:21.569
at this miasma, that they called it.

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:26.506
And the smell from all the poo and all
the animal waste was pretty horrible.

00:02:26.506 --> 00:02:28.339
And it was a pretty
sensible theory to think

00:02:28.339 --> 00:02:30.819
that that could be causing disease.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:33.939
But John Snow, who lived
not far from here--

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:36.349
we'll go there later--
was a physician in London,

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.029
who was convinced that cholera, in
particular, was a waterborne disease.

00:02:40.029 --> 00:02:43.229
It was not carried in this smelly air.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.199
And what happened here
on this spot in 1854

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:49.439
was that there was a baby who lived
at number 40 Broad Street, which

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:52.199
would have been just about
here, baby Lewis, who came down

00:02:52.199 --> 00:02:57.619
with some sort of terrible disease
that caused really terrible diarrhea.

00:02:57.619 --> 00:03:01.440
And I think pretty quickly, people
realized that it was cholera

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.689
and that there was going to be another
outbreak of this very terrible disease.

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:05.809
And people started dying.

00:03:09.509 --> 00:03:10.259
So what happens?

00:03:10.259 --> 00:03:14.729
What happened was the waste
from baby Lewis and other people

00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:18.749
who became ill with the cholera--
so human vehicle matter--

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:22.059
mixed with the water supply that
was in a relatively shallow well.

00:03:22.059 --> 00:03:25.749
I think it was about 20 feet
down under the street right here.

00:03:25.749 --> 00:03:28.669
And then the cholera
bacteria began to multiply.

00:03:28.669 --> 00:03:31.029
And then people would
ingest the water, which

00:03:31.029 --> 00:03:36.009
provides the human intestines a place
where the bacteria multiplied fast.

00:03:36.009 --> 00:03:38.509
They need a host like that.

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:42.179
So anyway, when people ingest
this contaminated water,

00:03:42.179 --> 00:03:47.529
they come down with cholera from
which you die in the hours to days.

00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:51.039
And John Snow, who really
was looking for a place

00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:54.339
where he could, unfortunate
circumstances as they were,

00:03:54.339 --> 00:03:56.839
have a very concentrated
outbreak of cholera

00:03:56.839 --> 00:04:00.749
where he could study the
origins of the outbreak,

00:04:00.749 --> 00:04:04.919
was interested in helping the people
but also interested immediately

00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:09.223
in collecting information about what
was going to happen in this outbreak.

00:04:09.223 --> 00:04:11.389
So he would have come over
here to this neighborhood

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:14.894
and started canvassing all the people
around here to see who was dying

00:04:14.894 --> 00:04:20.329
and who needed help and what they
had done to possibly ingest water

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.869
from the various water
supplies in the area.

00:04:22.869 --> 00:04:27.389
And right here, at a location
right near this red kerbstone,

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:32.009
was a source of what was apparently
some very clean-tasting-- sweet-tasting,

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:34.779
actually, water for drinking.

00:04:34.779 --> 00:04:37.369
Right here, it's called
the Broad Street Pump.

00:04:37.369 --> 00:04:40.369
And it turned out, as he started
canvassing the neighborhood,

00:04:40.369 --> 00:04:42.869
that he realized that most of
the people who had fallen ill

00:04:42.869 --> 00:04:46.738
and who were continuing to fall ill had
drunk water from the particular pump.

00:04:46.738 --> 00:04:49.029
So there were other water
problems in the neighborhood.

00:04:49.029 --> 00:04:50.799
And one of the things
that we'll see later

00:04:50.799 --> 00:04:54.239
is that, in the end, when he put all
these data together and he made a map,

00:04:54.239 --> 00:04:59.269
he had to show that this pump by
walking was the closest to almost all

00:04:59.269 --> 00:05:02.879
of the people who eventually
died-- 600-something people died

00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:04.089
in the epidemic.

00:05:04.089 --> 00:05:06.669
So even though there are other
pumps that are geographically

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:09.989
potentially nearer to those
people, this one tasted good

00:05:09.989 --> 00:05:14.589
and was close to the
people who died by walking.

00:05:14.589 --> 00:05:18.809
So in epidemiology today, it's known
that one way to really make your case

00:05:18.809 --> 00:05:21.769
is to have exceptions
to the rules-- so people

00:05:21.769 --> 00:05:24.509
who should have died, who didn't
die, and then people who did

00:05:24.509 --> 00:05:26.709
die for no apparent reason.

00:05:26.709 --> 00:05:28.939
And so John Snow, in
his work, actually found

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:30.679
both of those kinds of exceptions.

00:05:30.679 --> 00:05:34.979
And so one case is what people
sometimes refer to as the people who

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:35.999
were saved by the beer.

00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:39.699
And so these were the brewery workers
who worked a few blocks from here,

00:05:39.699 --> 00:05:45.059
and drank mostly beer, and so had
a clean supply of things to drink.

00:05:45.059 --> 00:05:47.879
And the other case was the
workhouse that was near here.

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:51.629
And these were the most
indigent potentially people

00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:52.649
in poorest of health.

00:05:52.649 --> 00:05:53.899
And so why did they live?

00:05:53.899 --> 00:05:56.639
They systematically
survived this epidemic.

00:05:56.639 --> 00:05:59.419
And part of the reason-- the main
and the most important reason--

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:04.089
is that the workhouse had a well, had
its own source of clean drinking water.

00:06:04.089 --> 00:06:07.289
So there are the exceptions of people
who should have died and didn't die.

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:09.659
And then what happened
was he also found out

00:06:09.659 --> 00:06:14.019
about a family who would bring
water to a member of that family who

00:06:14.019 --> 00:06:17.889
had moved far, a couple miles,
outside of this neighborhood.

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:20.779
And they brought her some
water, an elderly aunt.

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:23.089
And she and her niece drank it.

00:06:23.089 --> 00:06:24.559
And those people both died.

00:06:24.559 --> 00:06:28.739
So to us, looking at modern
epidemiological methods,

00:06:28.739 --> 00:06:33.359
John Snow had plenty of evidence to
say that this water from this well

00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:36.159
contained the contaminant
that caused the disease.

00:06:36.159 --> 00:06:41.190
But it still took actually many years
until the locals believed his story.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.939
And another thing that we should
mention is that John Snow did not

00:06:43.939 --> 00:06:45.360
collect all this data himself.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.109
If you look around the
street here, you'll

00:06:47.109 --> 00:06:49.119
see that this is a
very busy neighborhood

00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:50.579
with lots and lots of people.

00:06:50.579 --> 00:06:53.369
And I think it was even busier in 1854.

00:06:53.369 --> 00:06:56.039
But it was a small contained community.

00:06:56.039 --> 00:07:01.359
And there were people, for example
like a curate, Henry Whitehead, who was

00:07:01.359 --> 00:07:03.839
a local, who kind of knew everybody.

00:07:03.839 --> 00:07:08.519
And so John Snow worked with Whitehead
and with other people in the area

00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:10.859
to really canvas the
information and collect

00:07:10.859 --> 00:07:13.279
the kind of sociological
demographic information

00:07:13.279 --> 00:07:16.699
that he needed to know who lived where,
when they died, where they likely

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:20.909
got their water, who they talked
to, who they came in contact with,

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:23.829
all kinds of other details
about their personal lives.

00:07:23.829 --> 00:07:26.049
And so that kind of
human data collection,

00:07:26.049 --> 00:07:31.379
in this tightly knit community,
was also really, really important.

00:07:31.379 --> 00:07:35.929
[MUSIC PLAYING]

